WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.040
Good morning, John, it's Friday.

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.460
On Wednesday, I asked Twitter to pick between three different video topics

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.460
and boy were there a lot of people who didn't wanna hear about any of 'em!

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.380
But one of the tweets that got the most likes was this one:

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.300
"How about D) Puppies?"

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.500
So this video is entitled "Top 10 Best Dogs Ever."

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.160
And if you are unsatisfied with this content, then blame TheSteffie!

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.380
Or any of the 31 people who liked her tweet, including me.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:29.020
Let's begin, number 1: Stubby, a dog who served the allies in World War I.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.100
After being exposed to chemical weapons and then recovering in a hospital,

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.200
Stubby became attuned to the scent of these chemical weapons,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.340
and could alert soldiers before they could smell the gas coming.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.120
He was wounded twice, he served in 17 different battles,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.460
and he captured a German spy, recognizing him by his language,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.940
an act for which Stubby was promoted to sergeant, outranking his owner.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:57.240
Number 2: Lily, a pitbull who pulled her unconscious owner off of the railroad tracks

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.340
because there was a freight train coming.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.620
The owner did not get hit by the train, Lily did.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.820
The poor puppy lost one of her legs but survived

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.660
and if you think that story is remarkable, check out number 3:

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.380
First, dog trying to cross a highway gets hit by a car.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:18.160
Second, another dog, this hero dog, drags the dog that has been hit by the car off of the road,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.500
like, wanders out into the interstate, pulls the dog off,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.580
I'm not showing you the video because it's pretty graphic and terrifying,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.160
but I don't know, like, why is this dog doing this?

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.520
It's a, I mean, probably, I guess, just 'cause I was supposed to cry today?

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.740
Speaking of dogs rescuing people, and there are so many wonderful stories of dogs rescuing people,

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.260
we have this cat, who is clearly a cat, but is nonetheless, a very good dog.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.840
Number 5: In the 1950s, some Russian scientists went out onto the streets of Moscow

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:50.260
to collect stray dogs thinking, "These dogs, they are obviously used to extreme circumstances."

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:54.040
They tested and trained these dogs and one of them, Laika, was sent into space

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.440
and became the first animal ever to orbit Earth.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.460
Also, the first animal ever to die in orbit around the Earth.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:01.620
(grunts in frustration)

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.100
Not how we wanted that one to end.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.080
You know the first animal that went to space? It's a fruit fly.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.400
Number 6: Alvin and the Chipmunks.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.200
I know you think I'm crazy, but it's a little-known fact

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.360
that the actors who played Alvin and the Chipmunks in Alvin and the Chipmunks: The Squeakquel—

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:16.340
actually very small dogs!

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.660
Nope, number 7: Bummer and Lazarus,

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.980
who are two dogs, but we're just calling them one, sorry.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:26.120
These dogs didn't just survive mid-1800s San Francisco, where stray dogs outnumbered people

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.660
and were killed and poisoned regularly.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.240
They became nationally famous for their companionship with each other

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.020
and their ability to kill rats like crazy.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.600
Bummer and Lazarus were eventually exempted from local laws

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.860
and they showed up all the time in cartoons about San Francisco.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.160
They'd just sort of be there in the background.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.760
Number 8: This dog,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.120
which somebody sent me on Twitter.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.620
I asked people to send me dogs on Twitter, so I picked one. Real cute!

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.600
Good dog!

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.000
Number 9: Tweety Bird, who is a canary

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.740
and canaries are birds, but they are named after the Canary Islands

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.320
and the Canary Islands are named after the Latin word for dog.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.900
So, by the transitive property, Tweety Bird is a dog.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:05.720
Boom!

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.020
Number 10: My dog, Lemon, who passed away this year,

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.600
And as we all know, the best dog is your dog.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.180
And there's nothing wrong with that.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.600
Hey, I understand why a lot of people wanted me to talk about puppies today.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.220
It seems like we're in a much more tense moment than we were a couple of years ago,

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.900
and it does seem like that's gonna get worse before it gets better.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.240
We're being asked to pay attention to a lot of different big things all at the same time,

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.060
and the way that we're fed content about what's going on in the world right now

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.840
is inefficient, and it's overwhemling, and it's manipulative.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.340
Too frequently now, the people who are tasked with trying to inform the public are trying to win

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.140
rather than trying to inform,

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.620
and that is something that I'm getting super sick of.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.720
But there are still good dogs, and there are still good people.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.260
And in the coming years,

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:56.260
we can't choose between taking care of ourselves and taking care of our world's problems.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.900
We have to do both of those things.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.640
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

